          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c is 58.",
          "answer": "The information retrieved does not directly explain the clinical significance of an HbA1c value of 58 or provide diagnostic thresholds for this specific number.\n\nHowever, the documents do mention that HbA1c is used to monitor glycemic control, particularly in the context of Type 2 diabetes. Guidance often describes 7 percent as a default target, though individual goals can vary. It is important to note that HbA1c results can be unreliable if red cell turnover is altered, for example, in cases of hemoglobin variants, recent transfusions, iron-deficiency anemia, pregnancy, hemolysis, or advanced kidney disease. In such situations, glucose-based criteria are used instead (National diabetes association standards of care and preventive services guidance).\n\nYou should discuss this result with a clinician who can interpret it in the context of your individual health and medical history.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
